# Twitter Automation and Engagement

Lux provides transport-neutral Twitter/X automation helpers for scheduling,
rule-based engagement, queue management, content calendars, and action plans.

## Schedule Posts

```elixir
alias Lux.Integrations.Twitter.Automation

Automation.schedule_post("launch update", "2026-05-14T12:00:00Z", %{
  account_id: "account-1",
  tags: ["launch"]
})
```

## Queue Actions

```elixir
item =
  Automation.enqueue_action(:reply, %{
    tweet_id: "tweet-1",
    text: "Thanks for reaching out."
  })

Automation.transition(item, :completed, %{
  result: %{tweet_id: "reply-tweet"}
})
```

## Engagement Rules

```elixir
event = %{
  text: "please help with billing",
  like_count: 12,
  tweet_id: "tweet-1"
}

Automation.evaluate_rules(event, [
  %{
    id: "support-reply",
    conditions: %{
      text: %{operator: :contains, value: "billing"},
      like_count: %{operator: :>=, value: 10}
    },
    action: :reply,
    payload: %{
      reply_to_tweet_id: {:event, :tweet_id},
      text: "We can help."
    },
    priority: :high
  }
])
```

## Action Plans

```elixir
Automation.action_plan(:create_tweet, %{
  text: "Thanks!",
  reply_to_tweet_id: "tweet-1",
  idempotency_key: "reply:tweet-1"
})

Automation.action_plan(:repost, %{
  user_id: "user-1",
  tweet_id: "tweet-1"
})

Automation.action_plan(:follow, %{
  user_id: "user-1",
  target_user_id: "user-2"
})

Automation.action_plan(:direct_message, %{
  participant_id: "user-2",
  text: "hello"
})
```

Each action plan returns `%{action, method, endpoint, payload}` so an API client
can execute it while the scheduling and rule engine remain easy to test.

## Content Calendar

```elixir
Automation.calendar_summary([
  %{scheduled_at: "2026-05-14T12:00:00Z", state: :pending, tags: ["launch"]},
  %{scheduled_at: "2026-05-14T13:00:00Z", state: :completed, tags: ["docs"]}
])
```

Use the summary to build reports, review failed queue items, and rebalance
scheduled content across campaign days.
